ITEMS=[
('i','The order of 3y′+2y=5 is:',['1','2','3','4'],['The equation contains y and the first derivative y′.','There is no second or higher derivative.','Order records the highest derivative present.','The coefficient 3 does not make the order 3.','Therefore the order is 1.','Select (a).'],'(a) 1.'),
('ii','The degree of (y″)²−y′+y=0 is:',['1','2','3','4'],['The highest-order derivative is y″.','The equation is polynomial in its derivatives.','The highest derivative appears squared.','Hence its degree is 2, even though the lower derivative is linear.','Order is also 2 here, but order and degree measure different things.','Select (b).'],'(b) 2.'),
('iii','A solution depending on an arbitrary constant quantity is called:',['exact solution','particular','general solution','none of these'],['A family parametrized by an arbitrary constant is the general solution in this first-order context.','A particular solution fixes that constant using a condition.','An exact solution is an expression without numerical approximation, a different distinction.','The printed choices include the usual term general solution.','No constant is fixed in the stem.','Select (c).'],'(c) general solution.'),
('iv','The solution of y′=sin x at y(0)=1 is:',['y=sin x+2','y=−sin x+2','y=cos x+2','y=−cos x+2'],['Integrate sin x: y=−cos x+C.','At x=0, y=1 and cos0=1.','Thus 1=−1+C, giving C=2.','Therefore y=−cos x+2.','Differentiate to get sin x and evaluate y(0)=1.','Select (d).'],'(d) y=−cos x+2.'),
('v','The solution of yy′=cos x at y(0)=−1 is:',['y=−√(2sin x+1)','y=√(2sin x+1)','y=−√(2cos x+1)','y=√(2cos x+1)'],['Separate y dy=cos x dx.','Integrate y²/2=sin x+C.','At x=0,y=−1, C=1/2.','Hence y²=2sin x+1. Choose the negative square root to satisfy the initial sign.','The solution is y=−√(2sin x+1) on the interval (−π/6,7π/6) containing zero, where the radicand is positive.','At its zero endpoints yy′=cos x cannot hold with a finite derivative, since cos x is nonzero. Select (a).'],'(a) y=−√(2sin x+1), on the interval through zero with positive radicand.'),
('vi','The differential equation for orthogonal trajectories of x²+y²=c is:',['dy/dx=cx','y=cx','dy/dx=ct','cy=dy/dθ'],['Differentiate the circle equation: 2x+2yy′=0.','At points with finite nonzero slope, circle slope is −x/y.','The orthogonal slope is y/x, so the differential equation is y′=y/x.','Integrating gives radial lines y=Cx, with vertical x=0 handled separately.','Choice (b) gives the trajectory family, rather than the requested differential equation. None of the printed options is the correct general differential equation.','If the intended request was the finite-slope trajectory family, (b) is the intended answer; for the literal stem there is no correct option.'],'No correct differential-equation option; (b) is the finite-slope trajectory family.'),
('vii','Orthogonal trajectories of y²=4ax are:',['y²=k−2x²','x²=k−2y²','x²=k+2y²','y²=2x²−k'],['Differentiate with a fixed: 2yy′=4a.','Eliminate a using a=y²/(4x), giving y′=y/(2x) at regular points.','The orthogonal slope is −2x/y.','Separate y dy=−2x dx and integrate y²/2=−x²+C.','Thus y²+2x²=k, or y²=k−2x². Use k>0 for nondegenerate regular trajectories.','Select (a); horizontal/vertical cases follow from perpendicular tangent vectors.'],'(a) y²=k−2x².')
]
SUMMARY=[heading('Summary'),paragraph('A differential equation is an equation that involves the derivatives of an unknown function (dependent variable) of one or more variables (independent variables).'),paragraph('The order of a differential equation is the order of the highest-order derivative which appears in the equation.'),paragraph('The degree of a differential equation is the power of the highest-order derivative which appears in the equation.'),paragraph('A solution of an ordinary differential in one dependent variable y on an interval I is a function y(x) which, when substituted for the dependent variable y and its derivatives y′,y″,…, reduces the differential equation to an identity in the independent variable x over interval I.'),paragraph('A differential equation is linear in a set of one or more of its dependent variables if and only if each term of the equation which contains a variable of the set or any of its derivatives is of the first degree in those variables and their derivatives.'),paragraph('A function f(x,y) is homogeneous function of degree n in variables x and y if and only if for all values of the variables x,y and every positive λ, the identity is true:'),eq(r'f(\lambda x,\lambda y)=\lambda^n f(x,y),\quad n=1,2,3,\ldots'),paragraph('The differential equation:'),eq(r'\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{f(x,y)}{g(x,y)}'),paragraph('is called a homogeneous differential equation if it defines a homogeneous function of degree zero.'),note('Degree requires polynomial dependence on derivatives under the stated convention. For linearity, dependent variables and their derivatives appear only as a linear combination, with coefficients depending on independent variables; a product such as yy′ is nonlinear even though each factor separately has power one. Homogeneity of a first-order ratio is different from homogeneous linear equations. Homogeneous functions can also have degree zero or other real degrees on appropriate scaling domains; keep denominator and scaling-domain conditions.')]
HISTORY=[heading('History'),figure('ode299-jacob-bernoulli','Jacob Bernoulli (1654–1705)','Source portrait of Jacob Bernoulli.'),figure('ode299-johann-bernoulli','Johann Bernoulli (1667–1748)','Source portrait of Johann Bernoulli.'),paragraph('Jacob Bernoulli and Johann Bernoulli we two Swiss mathematicians. They were first interpreters of Leibniz’s version of differential equations. Both brothers orgue about Newton’s theories and maintained that the Newton’s theory of fluxions was plagiarized from the Leibniz’s original work. Both brothers worked hard using differential calculus to disprove Newton’s principle. The could not accept the theory which Newton had given, that earth and other plants roate around the sun in elliptical orbits. The first book on differential calculus was written on the bases of Bernoulli brothers ideas by Gabriele Manfred in 1707. Most of the publications were made on differential equations and partial differential equations in 18th century.'),note('The historical passage, including printed spellings and claims, is retained as source text. It is not independently verified history; its allegations should not be treated as established facts merely because the textbook states them.')]
